    "text_delta": "r for HR to sign off on the language.\n* Panel members frequently have demanding clinical and administrative schedules.\n* A delay is rarely a sign of an issue or bad faith; it is usually typical administrative bureaucracy.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Avoid Raising Gender or Panel Composition:** \n   Making an issue of the panel’s gender without concrete evidence would likely harm your professional reputation within the hospital network and could close doors to future internal promotions or collaborations.\n2. **Send a Polite, Forward-Looking Follow-Up:**\n   After two to three weeks, send a courteous email requesting a brief discussion or summary notes, framing it constructively:\n   > *\"I understand the panel has demanding schedules, but I would greatly appreciate any summary feedback or guidance when time permits. My goal is to understand which areas of my leadership profile or interview presentation to strengthen for future senior roles.\"*\n3. **Seek a Verbal Debrief if Possible:**\n   Panels are often cautious with written feedback due to legal sensitivities, which can lead to vague or generic statements. A 10-minute phone or in-person debrief often provides far more candid, actionable insights into what tipped the balance.\n",
